#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# array_plot

#% text
# `array_plot(m)` draws a matrix as cells, row 1 at the top: numbers from 0 (white) to 1 (black), colours as they are, `true` black and `false` white.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Numbers outside [0, 1] are scaled from the least to the greatest.

#% item
# Options: `color_function` (a gradient), `mesh: true` (lines between cells), and the options of `graphics`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A pattern of zeros and ones:

#% cell
array_plot([[1, 0, 1, 0], [0, 1, 0, 1], [1, 1, 0, 0]])
#% output
#= image("array_plot.files/array_plot-1.svgz")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Colours and booleans:

#% cell
array_plot([[red, blue, green], [true, false, true]])
#% output
#= image("array_plot.files/array_plot-2.svgz")

#% text
# The parity of binomial coefficients:

#% cell
array_plot(table(mod(binomial(i, j), 2), i, range(0, 31), j, range(0, 31)))
#% output
#= image("array_plot.files/array_plot-3.svgz")

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# A gradient and lines between cells:

#% cell
array_plot(table(i*j, i, 8, j, 8), color_function: viridis, mesh: true)
#% output
#= image("array_plot.files/array_plot-4.svgz")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `matrix_plot`, `raster`, `list_density_plot`
